#!/usr/bin/env briar
# Salutatio

A window that says hello. The program builds a small logical tree of
components, lowers one node of that tree into drawing commands, paints
the commands with the CPU rasterizer, and presents the result sixty
times a second until the window closes or six hundred frames have
gone by.

That is the whole program, and it is worth saying plainly before any
code appears, because the code cannot say it. C makes you write the
helpers before the thing that uses them, so a reader who starts at
the top of an ordinary `.c` file meets a rasterization callback with
no idea why it exists. Here the order is the reader's: the shape of
the program first, then each piece as the reader becomes ready for
it. briar generates a prototype for every function this file defines
and puts them in the derived header, so definition order carries no
weight with the compiler at all. The order is free, so it should be
spent on the reader.

The second thing this file does is teach its own vocabulary. Every
identifier here is Latin. A reader who knows the words reads the
program; a reader who does not is looking at noise. So the words are
glossed as they arrive, and the recurring ones are gathered first.

## Verba: the standing vocabulary

These appear in every rhubarb program and are not glossed again below.

| word | sense | note |
|---|---|---|
| *piscina* | fish-pond, basin, cistern | a Roman reservoir; in a church, the basin near the altar for washing the sacred vessels. Here: an arena. Memory is drawn from it and it is emptied whole. |
| *chorda* | a string, a cord | from Greek χορδή, the gut string of an instrument |
| *vacuum* | empty | `void` |
| *nihil* | nothing | `NULL` |
| *zephyrum* | zero | Fibonacci Latinizes Arabic *ṣifr* as *zephirum* in the *Liber Abaci* (1202); Italian *zefiro* wears down to *zero* |
| *redde* | give back! | imperative of *reddere*. *Reddite ergo quae sunt Caesaris Caesari* |
| *magnitudo* | greatness, size | `sizeof` |
| *probatio* | a testing, a proof | *probatio fidei vestrae*, 1 Pet. 1:7 |
| *interior* | inner | `static` at file scope: not for export |

Numeric constants are Roman numerals: `I`, `II`, `X`, `XX`, `L`, `C`,
`CXX`, `CCC`, `CCCXX`, `DC`, `IV`, `M`. So `IV * M * M` is four
mebibytes and `DC` is six hundred frames.

*Salutatio* is a greeting — third declension, genitive *salutationis*,
which is why the two functions below are named with the genitive:
`figura_salutationis` is *the figure of the greeting*, and
`componere_salutationis` is *the composing of the greeting*. The word
carries its own weight in the *salutatio angelica*, the Angelic
Salutation.

## Clausura: where the words come from

This file contains no `#include`. briar derives the closure from the
symbols used, which is a real convenience and a real cost: the reader
loses the one signal that tells him which library a name belongs to.
The table below pays that cost back. It is the reader's copy, not the
build's; `./salutatio.thistle -partes` prints the authoritative list,
and if the two ever disagree the table is wrong.

| header | what this file takes from it |
|---|---|
| `piscina.h` | the arena |
| `chorda.h`, `internamentum.h` | strings and the string interner |
| `componens.h` | the logical component tree, `Fines`, `PARTES_*` |
| `figura.h` | the figura registry: node kind → drawing function |
| `mandatum.h` | `Mandata`, `mandata_textus`, `ColorMandati` |
| `thema.h` | the colour theme, `COLOR_TEXT` |
| `insula.h`, `actio.h` | the island repository and the action registry |
| `dispensator.h` | the steward that rebuilds the tree each frame |
| `fenestra.h` | the native window and its pixel tablet |
| `ludus_fenestra.h` | the frame loop |
| `credo.h` | the test harness, in the probatio only |

## The shape of the program

Three units, in the order a reader wants them:

<c!>
<<#principale>>
<<#componere-salutationis>>
<<#figura-salutationis>>
</c>

## Principale: the whole program at a glance

*Principale* is the entry point — the *principal* thing, from
*princeps*, the one who takes first place.

The body is seven steps and nothing else. Each is named; each is
below. Read this and you have read the program; read the rest and you
know how it is done.

The declarations come first because C89 requires every declaration at
the head of its block. That constraint, which is usually a nuisance,
is here an asset: the block is the cast list. Ten objects, each named
for what it is, and a reader who takes the ten words in has taken in
the design.

<c! id="principale">
s32
principale (vacuum)
{
                Piscina* piscina;
    InternamentumChorda* intern;
     InsulaRepositorium* repo;
         ActioRegistrum* actiones;
        FiguraRegistrum* figurae;
            Dispensator* d;
               Fenestra* fenestra;
        TabulaPixelorum* tabula;
          LudusFenestra* lf;
   FenestraConfiguratio  cfg;
                    s32  exitus;

    <<#fundamentum>>
    <<#registra>>
    <<#figura-registrata>>
    <<#dispensator>>
    <<#fenestra>>
    <<#ansa>>
}
</c>

## Fundamentum: the ground

*Fundamentum*, a foundation, from *fundus*, the bottom of a thing.
*Internamentum* is not a classical word — it is coined from *internus*
for the string interner, which gives one canonical *chorda* per
distinct text so that later comparisons are pointer comparisons.
*Thema* is Greek, a thing set down: the colour theme.

Four mebibytes for the pool, taken once. Nothing below this line
calls `malloc`.

<c! id="fundamentum">
piscina = piscina_generare_dynamicum("salutatio", IV * M * M);
intern  = internamentum_creare(piscina);
thema_initiare();
</c>

## Registra: the three registries

*Registrum* is late Latin for a list or roll. *Insula* is an island,
and in Rome also an apartment block — a self-contained thing standing
on its own. *Actio* is a doing.

The repository holds the document and its ephemera as STML text; the
action registry holds what the user may do; the figura registry maps
a kind of node to the function that draws it.

<c! id="registra">
<<#repositorium>>
actiones = actio_registrum_creare(piscina, intern);
figurae  = figura_registrum_creare(piscina);
</c>

The repository line is written once and used twice — here and in the
probatio at the end of the file. It is one fragment with two windows,
not two copies that can drift apart.

<c! id="repositorium">
repo = insula_repositorium_creare(piscina, intern,
    "<documentum/>", "<ephemera/>");
</c>

## Figura registrata: where the two halves meet

One line, and the hinge of the whole program.

*Figura* is a shape or form. The word is doing more work here than it
looks: in patristic exegesis a *figura* is a type, an earlier thing
that carries the shape of a later one. That is exactly the office of
a figura in ludus. The component tree is logical and knows nothing of
pixels; a figura is what a node's shape becomes when it is brought
down into drawing commands. The tree is the type, the mandata are the
fulfilment.

This call says: when you meet a node of kind `PARTES_TITULUS`, draw it
with `figura_salutationis`.

<c! id="figura-registrata">
figura_registrare(figurae, PARTES_TITULUS, ZEPHYRUM,
                  figura_salutationis, NIHIL);
</c>

## Dispensator: the steward

*Dispensator* is a steward, the man who weighs out the household's
stores — *dispensatores mysteriorum Dei*, 1 Cor. 4:1. He does not own
what he hands out; he produces it on demand, in order, when asked.

That is the frame model. The dispensator is given the composing
function and calls it to rebuild the logical tree each frame. `CCC` is
its capacity in nodes.

<c! id="dispensator">
d = dispensator_creare(piscina, intern, repo, actiones,
                       componere_salutationis, NIHIL, CCC);
</c>

## Fenestra: the native window

*Fenestra*, a window — the same word as in *defenestration*.
*Vexilla* are military standards, the banners carried before a legion;
here, flag bits. *Vexilla regis prodeunt*.

The configuration struct is zeroed before use so that fields this
program does not set take their zero value rather than whatever was
on the stack. That is the C89 substitute for a designated initializer,
and it is the one place in this file where the language shows its age.

<c! id="fenestra">
memset(&cfg, ZEPHYRUM, magnitudo(FenestraConfiguratio));
cfg.titulus  = "salutatio";
cfg.x        = C;
cfg.y        = C;
cfg.latitudo = CCCXX;
cfg.altitudo = CXX;
cfg.vexilla  = FENESTRA_ORDINARIA;
fenestra = fenestra_creare(piscina, &cfg);
si (!fenestra)
{
    redde I;
}
</c>

## Ansa: the frame loop

*Ansa* is the handle of a vessel, the haft you take hold of — the
word rhubarb uses for a loop. *Tabula* is a board or tablet, as in the
*tabulae* of the Law; a `TabulaPixelorum` is the flat array of pixels
the rasterizer writes and the window presents.

`CXX` is the tablet's stride hint; `DC` is the frame cap, so the
program closes itself after six hundred frames whether or not anyone
closes the window. There is no `piscina_destruere` here: the pool dies
with the process, and pretending otherwise would be ceremony.

<c! id="ansa">
tabula = fenestra_creare_tabulam_pixelorum(piscina, fenestra, CXX);
lf = ludus_fenestra_creare(piscina, d, figurae, ZEPHYRUM, NIHIL, NIHIL,
                           tabula);
exitus = ludus_fenestra_currere(lf, fenestra, DC);
fenestra_destruere(fenestra);
redde exitus;
</c>

## Componere salutationis: building the logical tree

*Componere* is to put together, to compose; *componens* is its present
participle, *a thing putting together* — hence a component is not a
box but an act of composition caught mid-motion.

The dispensator calls this every frame. It builds two nodes: a root
that holds the window's extent, and a title node inside it. Nothing
here knows what a pixel is.

*Fines* are boundaries or limits, always plural — *fines terrae*, the
ends of the earth. *Radix* is a root: *O Radix Iesse*.

<c! id="componere-salutationis">
Componens*
componere_salutationis (
     InsulaRepositorium* repo,
        constans Motus* motus,
               Piscina* p,
    InternamentumChorda* in,
                vacuum* ctx)
{
    Componens* radix;
    Componens* titulus;
         Fines f;

    (vacuum)repo;
    (vacuum)motus;
    (vacuum)ctx;
    <<#componens-radix>>
    <<#componens-titulus>>
    redde radix;
}
</c>

The root fills the window and draws nothing: `PARTES_NULLUM`, the kind
that is no part at all.

<c! id="componens-radix">
radix = componens_creare(p, in, "radix", PARTES_NULLUM);
f.x        = ZEPHYRUM;
f.y        = ZEPHYRUM;
f.latitudo = CCCXX;
f.altitudo = CXX;
componens_ponere_fines(radix, f);
</c>

The title node is inset and carries the text. *Titulus* is an
inscription — the *titulus* is the board Pilate had nailed above the
Cross. *Ponere* is to place or set. *Liberum* is a child: *liberi* are
one's children, so `componens_addere_liberum` reads *add a child*, not
*add a free thing*.

<c! id="componens-titulus">
titulus = componens_creare(p, in, "salutatio", PARTES_TITULUS);
f.x        = X;
f.y        = L;
f.latitudo = CCC;
f.altitudo = XX;
componens_ponere_fines(titulus, f);
componens_ponere_titulum(titulus, "salve, munde");
componens_addere_liberum(radix, titulus);
</c>

## Figura salutationis: lowering the node into commands

*Mandata* are things commanded, neuter plural of *mandatum* — *si
diligitis me, mandata mea servate*. A `Mandata` is the list of drawing
orders for one frame. This function turns one logical node into one
such order: put this text at this offset in this colour.

The colour is named by theme rather than by value, so the same tree
draws light or dark without touching this function. Nothing is drawn
from the pictor here — no volumen, no sqlite, so the closure stays
small.

<c! id="figura-salutationis">
vacuum
figura_salutationis (
    constans Componens* c,
              Mandata* m,
                  i32  thema,
              vacuum* ctx)
{
    ColorMandati color;

    (vacuum)thema;
    (vacuum)ctx;
    color.genus = COLOR_MANDATI_THEMA;
    color.valor = (i32)COLOR_TEXT;
    mandata_textus(m, II, II, c->titulus, ZEPHYRUM, color);
}
</c>

## Probatio

The probatio does not open a window. It runs the composing function
directly and checks that the tree came out with the title node in it
and the right text on that node — the part of this program that can be
judged without a screen in front of it.

Note the shared `#repositorium` fragment: the probatio builds its
repository with the same line the program does, because if that line
ever changes the test must change with it.

<c! munus="probatio">
s32
principale (vacuum)
{
                Piscina* piscina;
    InternamentumChorda* intern;
     InsulaRepositorium* repo;
              Componens* arbor;
              Componens* c;

    piscina = piscina_generare_dynamicum("probatio", LXIV * M);
    credo_aperire(piscina);
    intern = internamentum_creare(piscina);
    <<#repositorium>>
    arbor = componere_salutationis(repo, NIHIL, piscina, intern, NIHIL);
    CREDO_NON_NIHIL(arbor);
    c = componens_invenire_per_id(arbor,
                                  chorda_ex_literis("salutatio", piscina));
    CREDO_NON_NIHIL(c);
    CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(c->titulus, "salve, munde");
    credo_imprimere_compendium();
    redde credo_omnia_praeterierunt() ? ZEPHYRUM : I;
}
</c>

## Consilia: what was considered and not done

A literate program that only says what it does is half a document.
These are the choices behind the code, and the places a later reader
will stub his toe.

**The frame cap.** `DC` frames is a measurement artifact: this file
was first written to time a run, and a program that closes itself is
easier to time than one that waits. The alternative — run until the
window closes — is one argument away and is what a real application
would want. The cap stays because an example that never exits is a
bad example.

**The plain-program form, not the window region.** briar offers a
`<fenestra>` region with bridge methods, which drives the vitrea web
view. This file deliberately uses the plain form and calls
`fenestra_creare` itself, because the point is to exercise ludus down
to the rasterizer. A thistle file that wanted a web UI would look
nothing like this one.

**Known gaps in the derivation.** Three were found when this program
was first built by hand and are worth carrying here rather than
losing in a commit message. The plain form needs `.m` files and
`-framework Cocoa` in its generated `aedificare.sh`. The closure for
`fenestra_textus`, the font path, and utf8 is not complete. And a
function passed as a value is not derived — which bites this file
exactly once, at `figura_salutationis` in `#figura-registrata`, where
the name is used but never called. That last one is the interesting
failure: derivation follows calls, and a callback is a use that is
not a call.

**What fragments did not solve.** C89 forces every declaration to the
head of its block, so `#principale` cannot hand each object to the
step that uses it — the cast list has to arrive whole. Knuth's
stepwise refinement wants the declaration beside the use, and C89
will not give it. Nothing in briar can fix that; it is the language.
